I would like to make a music composer app. Is it possible to actaully generate tunes. Whaty wpuld it ttake
Yes—you can build an app that actually generates original tunes, plays them, and lets you edit them. The key choice is whether you want an editable melody or a finished audio track with instruments and vocals. Those require quite different systems.

For your interest in composing and working in Logic Pro, I would start with an editable melody composer.

Imagine this workflow:

Choose A minor, 3/4 time, 80 BPM, and a gentle mood.
Enter a chord progression such as Am → F → C → E.
Click Generate to hear three different eight-bar melodies.
Keep the phrases you like and regenerate the others.
Export MIDI into Logic Pro to choose instruments and develop the arrangement.
The app could also accept a melody you play on your Yamaha keyboard and generate a continuation or variations.

How would it generate a tune?

A melody is a sequence of notes, each with a pitch, start time, duration, and velocity. The app generates that sequence, then a synthesizer plays it.

There are three practical approaches:

Approach	How it works	Main trade-off
Musical rules	Generates notes using scales, chords, rhythm patterns, repetition, and phrase endings	Easy to control; can sound predictable
Trained music model	Uses a model that has learned patterns from music	Can produce richer ideas; integration and compute are more involved
Hybrid	Combines musical constraints with model-generated ideas	Offers a useful balance of creativity and control
The important part is generating phrases, rather than choosing individual notes at random. For example, an eight-bar tune could introduce an idea in bars 1–2, repeat it with a small change in bars 3–4, develop it in bars 5–6, and resolve it in bars 7–8. That structure helps produce the logical flow you are looking for.

For an initial version, I would build:

Component	Purpose
Browser interface with a piano roll	View, move, and resize notes
Composition engine	Generate rhythm, motifs, variations, and chord-aware melodies
Audio playback	Hear the melody and chord accompaniment
MIDI import/export	Exchange editable music with Logic Pro
Phrase controls	Lock good sections and regenerate selected bars
A React interface and a Python composition engine would fit your existing skills. A rules-based first version could also run entirely in the browser, without paid AI calls or a GPU.

As rough planning estimates for one experienced developer:

Basic proof of concept: a few focused days—generate and play a melody over supplied chords.
Useful personal app: approximately 2–4 weeks—piano-roll editing, variations, saving, and MIDI export.
Polished product: several months—better musical quality, reliable playback, recording, and extensive usability work.
Generating a complete song as audio is a separate, larger step. It would generally involve integrating an existing audio-generation model or service; training your own from scratch would be a much bigger undertaking.

My suggested first target: “Generate three coherent eight-bar melodies over my chords, let me edit them, and export to Logic Pro.” It is achievable, useful for your own composing, and gives you a clear way to judge whether the app produces music you like.

How will it create music. What is the app trained on?
The first version I suggested would not need to be trained on anything. We would program musical principles into it. If we later add a trained AI model, its training data would depend on the model we choose.

There are two distinct ways to create music.

1. Compose using musical rules—no training required

The app would construct a melody from your chosen key, chords, and rhythm:

Step	Example in A minor
Choose chords	Am → F → C → E
Create a rhythm	A mix of quarter notes, eighth notes, and rests
Pick notes that fit	Over Am, favour A, C, and E on strong beats
Connect those notes	Use nearby scale notes between chord tones
Create a short motif	A recognisable two-bar musical idea
Develop the motif	Repeat it, change its ending, or shift its pitches
End the phrase	Resolve toward A for a sense of arrival
For example, a simple starting phrase could be:

Over Am: A–C–E
Over F: A–G–F
Over C: E–G–E
Over E: D–B–G♯
Back to Am: A held

The G♯ over E helps pull the melody back toward A. Rhythm, note lengths, and rests would determine how this actually sounds.

The app would generate several candidates and score them against criteria we define: chord fit, manageable melodic leaps, repetition, variety, and convincing endings. These scores measure our musical preferences; they cannot guarantee a beautiful tune.

2. Use an AI model trained on music

For editable melodies, a model can be trained on symbolic music, such as MIDI or encoded scores.

A training example contains information like:

Note A4 starts at beat 1, lasts half a beat, and has velocity 80; note C5 follows…

Across many examples, the model learns patterns involving:

Which notes and rhythms tend to follow one another.
How melodic phrases repeat and change.
How melodies relate to harmony, when the training representation includes it.
How musical passages develop and finish.
During generation, it predicts successive musical events, conditioned on whatever inputs it supports—for example, an opening melody or chord progression. A synthesizer then turns those events into sound.

An audio-generation model instead learns from recordings, sometimes paired with descriptions. That is useful for producing finished-sounding audio, but extracting clean, editable notes and separate instruments is a different challenge.

What would your app be trained on?

We have not selected a model or dataset yet, so there is no training dataset to name.

Build choice	Training requirement
Rules-based composer	None—we write the composition logic
Existing pretrained model	Uses that model’s existing training; we would check its documentation and licence
Custom-trained model	Requires a suitable collection of licensed, public-domain, or otherwise permitted music
Personalised model	Could adapt an existing model using your own compositions, if enough suitable material is available
I would start with musical rules, motif development, and MIDI output. That would let you test whether the app helps you compose. We could then add a pretrained model if you want greater variety and more sophisticated phrase development.

Which pretrained models are there
Yes—there are pretrained models you can download and build into your app. For your composer, I would focus on models that generate MIDI, so you can edit the notes and use them in Logic Pro.

Here is a practical shortlist, including what they were trained on.

Model	What it generates	Training data	Potential use in your app
Aria	Expressive piano music as MIDI; continuation of an existing passage	Approximately 60,000 hours of MIDI transcriptions of solo-piano recordings	Play a short piano idea and have the app continue it. Primarily suited to piano.
Anticipatory Music Transformer — Stanford	MIDI generation conditioned on supplied musical events	Lakh MIDI dataset for the original models	Generate music around notes you supply; useful for accompaniment and controlled completion.
Text2MIDI	MIDI from a written description	SymphonyNet for pretraining and MidiCaps for caption-based fine-tuning	Type “a gentle piano melody in A minor” and get editable music.
These capabilities and training sources are documented by the respective projects. Text prompts can guide generation, but exact compliance with every requested chord, tempo, or phrase length needs testing. 

There are also models that generate sound directly:

Model	What it does	Training data	Main consideration
MusicGen / MusicGen Melody — Meta	Generates music audio from text; the Melody variant also accepts a melody reference	About 20,000 hours of licensed music, including Shutterstock and Pond5 material	Useful for hearing an arranged interpretation of an idea; output is audio rather than editable MIDI.
Stable Audio Open 1.0	Generates audio from descriptions	486,492 recordings from Freesound and Free Music Archive	More suitable for sounds, textures, and samples; its own documentation says it performs better on sound effects than music.
MusicGen’s released weights have a noncommercial licence, which matters if you intend to sell or commercially host the app. 

My recommendation is to test Aria and Text2MIDI first, because they support two useful versions of your idea:

Aria: “Here are two bars I played. Continue this musical idea.”
Text2MIDI: “Create a gentle piano piece from this description.”
For your original goal—eight bars over Am → F → C → E, with editable phrases—we would still add composition controls around the model to enforce length, check harmony, and let you regenerate sections. A pretrained model provides the musical suggestions; the app makes those suggestions controllable and useful.


